[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Hi, my name\'s Adam. I am the co-founder of Relume and I\'d like to take this opportunity to introduce you to the beta version of Relume 2.0. Now, this is a early demo of the product. We are still fixing things before launch,'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'but I thought it\'d be a good opportunity to record something, put together a bit of a walk-through of me going through the project or a project and sort of walking you through how things work and why we\'ve decided to create Relume the'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'way it is. Now, we believe that what we\'ve created is quite different to what already exists in the website builder space and it really the ideas come from our own experiences building marketing websites over the last 10 years and even being an agency before we turned into a'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'product company at Relume. And so, what we\'ve tried to do is just build a product that we would want to use and put that out in the world. So, I think the best way to actually sort of explain the philosophy behind the product is to just use it. So, let\'s start with the'

[00:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'first screen. So, when you open a project, what we\'re encouraging you to do here is just to dump as much context as possible into this screen. So, I have a made-up company website brief. Let\'s'

[01:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'just imagine that there was this company that created a brief for me. I\'m going to add that in. Um I\'ve also got some nice images we\'ve put together here. I\'m going to add say like a few of them in'

[01:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'here as well and I think for now that\'s probably enough. Back to what this screen is is supposed to do. We didn\'t want to design experience where you went back and forth in a questionnaire chat with AI. Like that\'s just that doesn\'t'

[01:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'map to our reality. Our reality is by the time we\'ve opened a file and we started working on a project, we\'ve probably had a bunch of conversations with a client or, you know, if if we\'re building for our own website, we probably had a bunch of'

[01:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'meetings about the project and we probably we probably have a bunch of different docs lying around and if AI is going to create anything, it\'s going to need really good context. Otherwise, it\'s just a waste of time. And so, we\'re really encouraging you to dump as much'

[01:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'information as possible here because the next step is that AI is going to pull it into what we call the brief. So, I\'m going to go and just add this and then I\'ll explain to you what the brief is. You can also paste the URL. That actually works quite well if you want to'

[02:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'do a redesign of of a website. And instead of skipping straight to the design, I\'m going to actually start from the brief. Okay. So, back to the brief. So, what\'s AI doing here? Well, AI is taking all the context you\'ve just dumped and it\'s going to create a brief'

[02:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'for itself that becomes the source of truth. This becomes the context that it uses to make decisions downstream. And the decisions that it makes is based on a workflow which which I\'ll take you through. So, as you can see here, a'

[02:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'brief overview doc has been created and there are a bunch of supporting docs that it\'s pulled in. Now, if I open the supporting doc, this is a doc about the brand\'s visuals and then let\'s look at another doc. Maybe there\'s the target audience. This is'

[02:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'some information about the target audience. And so, it puts it into these docs so that it can reference it when it goes ahead and and creates the project. So, it creates the designs. All right. So, once we have the brief in a good spot. Oh, by the way, just before we'

[02:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'jump in, these are just markdown files. You can update them. You can add in your own docs if you want AI to refer to them and you can point AI back to them as well. All right. Let\'s move on to generating a sitemap. So, the next step in the process is to generate the'

[03:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sitemap. Really 1.0 already has the sitemap view so we\'ve just carried this this view over. We still think it\'s a really useful view and useful part of the process. Before you jump into designing the pages, you want to have a view that sort of maps out all the pages'

[03:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that are needed. This can help you scope project. This can help you sort of organize information on the site and within a page before you jump ahead into designing the page itself. So, as you can see here, the AI has'

[03:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'created the sitemap. It\'s It\'s mentioned that it\'s following the brief. I think in the brief we had to put certain amount of pages so it\'s followed that. It\'s now going to update the home page to include all the sections that are'

[03:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'needed, and it stops. It stops there. It doesn\'t go any further. It doesn\'t go ahead and generate everything at once. We didn\'t want AI to run away from you. We want it to meet you where you are, and if I want, I can generate the remaining pages, but for the purpose of this demo, I\'m going to move on to the'

[04:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'next step, which is the wireframe view, and which is where I believe Relume really shines and is quite different to other website builders. The analogy I like to use is the Lego analogy. Think about Relume as'

[04:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'creating Lego a Lego Lego pieces for AI to assemble. And that\'s different to say using say code design, which is actually writing the code uh for you on the on the fly.'

[04:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, it\'s the way it arrives at the design is actually to generate the code for it. Whereas what Relume\'s doing is it\'s assembling the in our Lego factory by real humans. And our goal and our vision is for every Lego piece to be and'

[04:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that can mean built best practices with accessibility, SEO, machine interpretability, all all good, but also built with the right spacing, built with layouts that support content. And so,'

[05:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'what\'s happening here is that we in the background AI is generating the content, and then it\'s finding the most appropriate layouts to support that content. And so, what we\'re trying to do'

[05:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is build a component system that can do that job effectively. Also, we like to use the word unbreakable. We want to be We want our Lego pieces to be unbreakable, meaning that you shouldn\'t be able to arrive at any instance that where the layout'

[05:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'breaks. And that just means that you can edit things yourself really effectively, but you can also hand it over to clients to edit things themselves, and things just won\'t break because the Lego pieces don\'t enable that to happen. Another'

[05:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'thing is that they will just work across different breakpoints out of the box. And so, there are many benefits and advantages of taking this approach. Not only is it faster, but it leads to default great outcomes that that are great for that look good and are'

[05:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'unbreakable. But, also every single website that exists on Relume is built on the same component library. So, over time this component library is going to improve, and as more websites'

[06:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'get built on this component library and we get more feedback from the from the community, it\'s only going to get better and and everyone can benefit and everyone can share in the benefits of that, which is really cool and sort of what we\'re aiming to do here. Now, this'

[06:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is V1. Our components are this is the starting point, but we plan we have big vision of of where we want to take these layouts. We want to bring back our uncommon components. We want to introduce Bento grids. We want to'

[06:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'perfect a slider. We want to do all these things. This is literally just the starting point for us. We\'re really excited for what, you know, the future holds as well and receiving feedback around this and improving that system so everyone can benefit. All right, so as'

[06:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you can see here, we\'ve created a wireframe and the wireframe view essentially we\'ve kept the wireframe view because we want to create a really focused experience where you\'re not worrying about the visuals. You\'re not distracted by them. You\'re fully focused'

[06:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on the information and the content and the layouts and that\'s all. And this also provides like a really good view for you to share with clients as and a view where they can just focus in on the content and and ignore everything else. So, that\'s why we\'ve kept the wireframe'

[07:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'view and as you can see here, AI has put together a bit of a wireframe. So, I\'m going to jump into some of the editing experience. Now, I could spend, you know, another I could spend hours talking about this, but I\'m going to try'

[07:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and get through this as quick as possible. All right, so if you go to site, we have our layers panel and within every component, we have a bunch of smaller components. So, these smaller components you can toggle'

